.TH TAILMON 1 "October 2026" "tailmon" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tailmon \- agentless CPU, GPU, RAM, IO and disk monitor for the machines on a Tailscale tailnet
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tailmon
.RB [ \-w
.IR SEC ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-log
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-quiet ]
.RB [ \-\-hosts
.IR LIST ]
.br
.B tailmon search
.RI [ PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR GLOB ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-\-hosts
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-exclude
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-results
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-hidden ]
.RB [ \-\-proc ]
.RB [ \-\-boom ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR SEC ]
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tailmon grep
.I PATTERN
.RI [ PATH ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-\-fixed\-strings ]
.RB [ \-\-hosts
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-exclude
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-results
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-hidden ]
.RB [ \-\-proc ]
.RB [ \-\-boom ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR SEC ]
.RB [ \-v | \-vv ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tailmon mount
.RB [ \-\-path
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-hosts
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tailmon umount
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-hosts
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tailmon gate
.BR install | approve | status | remove | hashes
.RB [ \-\-hosts
.IR LIST ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tailmon
shows CPU, memory, GPU, disk and network throughput, filesystem usage, logged\-in users
and SLURM state for every online Linux machine on your Tailscale network.
.PP
Hosts are discovered with
.BR "tailscale status \-\-json" .
Each online Linux peer is probed over
.BR ssh (1)
by piping a small, standard\-library\-only Python script to
.B "python3 \-"
on the remote side. Nothing is installed, copied or left behind on the monitored hosts.
The local machine is probed directly, without SSH.
.PP
Non\-Linux and offline peers are listed as \(lqnot probed\(rq.
.PP
The
.B search
and
.B grep
subcommands use the same mechanism to find files by name, or lines by content,
on all online Linux peers at once.
The
.B mount
subcommand mounts every peer's filesystem under one local directory with
.BR sshfs (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-watch " " \fISEC\fR
Live view, refreshed every
.I SEC
seconds. One persistent SSH session per host streams samples; dropped connections are
retried every 10 seconds, and the tailnet is re\-scanned every
.B discover_seconds
(see
.BR CONFIGURATION )
so that hosts joining or leaving are picked up. Ignored with
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a single JSON snapshot (an array with one object per host) and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " FILE"
Append each sample to
.I FILE
as JSON Lines, one object per host per sample. Combine with
.B \-w
to keep a history.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Do not draw the table. Meant for use with
.B \-\-log
or
.B \-\-alert
from
.BR cron (8)
or
.BR systemd (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-alert " CMD"
Run the shell command
.I CMD
each time a temperature sensor reaches its limit (once, until it cools down and heats up again).
Its environment holds
.BR TAILMON_HOST ,
.BR TAILMON_SENSOR ,
.BR TAILMON_CHIP ,
.B TAILMON_KIND
(cpu, gpu, disk, other),
.BR TAILMON_TEMP ,
.B TAILMON_LIMIT
and
.BR TAILMON_LEVEL .
In snapshot mode it runs for every hot sensor. See
.BR TEMP " under " OUTPUT .
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " LIST"
Only probe these comma\-separated Tailscale hostnames.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS "search [PATH]"
Find regular files whose name matches a glob under
.I PATH
(default: the remote user's home directory, i.e.\&
.BR . ).
Symbolic links to directories are not followed. Output is one
.IB host ": " path
line per match.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " GLOB"
Shell\-style filename pattern, e.g.\&
.BR \(aq*.log\(aq .
Default:
.BR * .
Quote it so your local shell does not expand it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Match
.I GLOB
case\-insensitively, so
.B \(aq*.pdf\(aq
also finds
.IR REPORT.PDF .
.SS "grep PATTERN [PATH]"
Search the contents of text files under
.I PATH
(default
.BR . )
for lines matching
.IR PATTERN ,
a Python regular expression
(see
.BR re
in the Python documentation, not POSIX
.BR regex (7)).
Files whose first 8 KiB contain a NUL byte are treated as binary and skipped.
Output is one
.IB host : path : line ": " text
line per match.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Match case\-insensitively.
.TP
.B \-\-fixed\-strings
Treat
.I PATTERN
as literal text rather than a regular expression.
.SS "Options common to search and grep"
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " LIST"
Only search these comma\-separated Tailscale hostnames.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " LIST"
Do not search these comma\-separated Tailscale hostnames (this machine included, if named),
e.g. a host whose large disks would make the whole search wait. To leave a host out of every
command for good, use the
.B exclude
configuration key instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-results " N"
Stop after
.I N
results. Default: 1000.
.TP
.B \-\-hidden
Include files and directories whose name starts with a dot (skipped by default).
.TP
.B \-\-proc
Also walk into
.I /proc
when searching a directory above it, such as
.IR / .
It is skipped by default: its pseudo\-files are endless and rarely what you want.
Giving
.I /proc
itself as
.I PATH
searches it either way.
.TP
.B \-\-boom
Disable the loop guard. By default the walk does not enter sshfs mounts below
.I PATH
(such as
.IR /media/tailscale ,
where peers mounted with
.B tailmon mount
can mount each other again, without end) and never reads the same directory twice
(bind mounts of a parent). An sshfs mount given as
.I PATH
itself is searched either way.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print
.B {"results": [...], "errors": [...], "skipped": [...]}
instead of text.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SEC"
Give up on a host after
.I SEC
seconds; what it found until then is kept, and the error names the path it was reading
(with
.BR \-vv ).
.B 0
means no limit. Default: 300.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Once: after the search, print one line per host on standard error with the number of matches,
the time taken, the walk rate (directories plus files read per second, e.g.
.BR 12.3k/s ),
the directories and files walked and the depth reached (the deepest directory level
below the root, which is 0) (also added as
.B stats
to the
.B \-\-json
output).
Twice
.RB ( \-vv ):
while searching, also show one line per host on standard error, redrawn in place, with a progress
bar, the number of matches, the elapsed time, the walk rate so far, the current depth and the deepest reached so far
(e.g.
.BR "depth 3/7" )
and the directory (for grep: the file) being read;
when the same path has been read for 3 seconds or more, its duration is shown, which points at slow
disks or huge files. The bar is an estimate: each directory splits its parent's share evenly among
its subdirectories. Each host then reports its position every 0.5 seconds when it changed, which costs some
network traffic. The display is only drawn when standard error is a terminal; the board is erased
before the results are printed.
.PP
Per\-host errors and the list of hosts that were not searched are written to standard error.
.SS "volume [LEVEL]"
Show the sound volume of every host, or change it:
.I LEVEL
is a percentage
.RB ( 40 ),
a step
.RB ( +5 ", " \-5 ),
.BR mute ", " unmute " or " toggle .
The default output is driven with
.BR wpctl ", " pactl ", " amixer
or, on macOS,
.BR osascript ,
whichever works, so the user's sound server must be running.
Windows hosts (with the OpenSSH server) are driven through PowerShell and the Core Audio API;
Android hosts need Termux with sshd, python and Termux:API, and get their media volume set.
Takes
.BR \-\-hosts ", " \-\-exclude " and " \-\-json .
.SS "serve"
Serve a web page with a volume slider and mute button per host, and a JSON API
.RB ( "GET /api/volume" ,
.B POST /api/volume/HOST
with
.BR "{\(dqaction\(dq: \(dqset\(dq, \(dqvalue\(dq: 40}" ).
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ADDR"
Address to listen on. Default:
.BR 0.0.0.0 ,
every interface, with no password; give this PC's Tailscale IP to limit it to the tailnet.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
Default: 8765.
.TP
.BI \-\-token " TOKEN"
Require
.BI ?token= TOKEN
in the URL (or an
.B X\-Token
header).
.TP
.BR \-\-hosts ", " \-\-exclude
Hosts to offer, as for
.BR volume .
.SS "mount"
Mount each online Linux and macOS peer with
.BR sshfs (1)
at
.IB ROOT / host
(default root:
.IR /media/tailscale ),
so local programs can work on files from all machines at once.
The local machine gets a symbolic link instead of a mount.
Hosts that are already mounted are left alone; stale mounts of hosts that went away are
replaced. Mounts use the same SSH settings as the probes, run as your SSH user, are
read\-write, and reconnect after network drops. They do not survive a reboot.
.TP
.BI \-\-path " DIR"
Remote directory to mount. Default:
.BR / .
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
Local directory that holds one mount per host. It must be writable by you; for the default, create it once with
.BR "sudo install \-d \-o $USER \-g $(id \-gn) /media/tailscale" .
.SS "umount"
Unmount every mount (and remove every symbolic link) that
.B mount
created under the root. Takes
.B \-\-root
as above.
.SS "Options common to mount and umount"
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " LIST"
Only these comma\-separated Tailscale hostnames.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print
.B {"results": [...], "skipped": [...]}
instead of text.
.SS "gate install|approve|status|remove|hashes"
Let each host approve the scripts
.B tailmon
runs on it. Linux, macOS and Android (Termux) hosts; Windows hosts keep your normal login.
.TP
.B install
Log in once with your normal key; create
.B tailmon\(aqs
own key
.RB ( gate_key ,
default
.IR ~/.ssh/tailmon_gate )
here if needed; on each host, copy the gate to
.IR ~/.local/share/tailmon/gate.py ,
approve this version\(aqs scripts (monitoring probe, file tool, volume) in
.IR ~/.config/tailmon/approved ,
back up
.I ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
to
.I authorized_keys.before\-tailmon
and add the key to it as
.BR "restrict,command=\(dqPYTHON GATE\(dq" ,
with the absolute path of the
.B python3
that the host\(aqs login shell finds. Then check that the key reaches the gate, and add the host to
.BR gate_hosts .
.TP
.B approve
Approve this version\(aqs scripts on the gated hosts (needed after updating
.BR tailmon ).
.TP
.B status
Ask each gate, with the gate key only, whether it runs this version\(aqs scripts.
.TP
.B remove
Delete the key line and the gate from the hosts and take them out of
.BR gate_hosts .
.TP
.B hashes
Print the SHA\-256 of this version\(aqs scripts, to approve them by hand.
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " LIST"
Default: every online Linux, macOS and Android host for
.BR install ,
the gated hosts otherwise.
.PP
The gate runs a script only when its SHA\-256 is approved. Anything else (a shell, another program, an
unapproved script) exits with status 77 and is logged in
.IR ~/.config/tailmon/gate.log ;
.B restrict
also forbids forwarding, agent and terminals.
.B tailmon
reaches gated hosts with the gate key only
.RB ( IdentitiesOnly=yes ", " ControlPath=none )
and a command that starts with
.BR tailmon\-gate ,
which only the gate accepts, so a host that lets in another key fails instead of running the script
unchecked. On a host, its owner can run
.B gate.py list
(approved scripts, latest log),
.BI "gate.py approve " SHA256
.RI [ name ]
and
.BI "gate.py revoke " SHA256
with the
.B python3
of the key line.
.B mount
keeps using your normal key: SFTP can\(aqt be narrowed down.
.SH OUTPUT
For each host the table shows a header line (name, core count, load average) followed by:
.TP 8
.B CPU
Utilization over the sample interval, from
.IR /proc/stat .
.TP
.B RAM
Used memory (total minus
.BR MemAvailable )
and swap, from
.IR /proc/meminfo .
.TP
.B GPU
Per NVIDIA GPU: utilization and memory, when
.BR nvidia\-smi (1)
is available.
.TP
.B IO
Disk read/write throughput from
.I /proc/diskstats
(physical disks only) and network rx/tx from
.I /proc/net/dev
(excluding loopback, docker, veth, bridges and tailscale interfaces).
.TP
.B HDD
Usage of local filesystems of at least 1 GiB (ext*, xfs, btrfs, zfs, ...), with inode usage
when the filesystem reports it, plus a per\-host total.
.TP
.B TEMP
The hottest reading of each temperature sensor chip, from
.I /sys/class/hwmon
(or
.I /sys/class/thermal
when there is none) and
.BR nvidia\-smi (1):
CPU (CPU0, CPU1 per socket), GPU0 ..., disks by name (nvme0, sda) and board chips by driver name.
Each is compared with the limit the sensor reports (its max, or crit \- 10), else 85\(deC for
CPUs and GPUs, 65\(deC for disks and 80\(deC for the rest, or the
.B temp_high
setting. It is yellow within
.B temp_warn_margin
of the limit and red at it; such sensors are also listed, worst first, in a HOT or WARM line at
the top. SATA disks report a temperature only with the kernel's
.B drivetemp
module loaded.
.TP
.B TOTAL
Storage summed over all hosts. Shown only when at most one Linux host is offline or
unreachable, in which case it is marked incomplete. Excluded and non\-Linux peers do not count.
.TP
.B USERS
Interactive logins from
.BR who (1);
people other than the SSH user are highlighted.
.TP
.B SLURM
Node state, CPU allocation and the job queue from
.BR sinfo (1)
and
.BR squeue (1),
when installed (refreshed at most every 10 seconds). Hosts reporting the same cluster show it once.
.PP
Bars and percentages are green below 70%, yellow from 70% and red from 90%.
Colors are only used when standard output is a terminal.
.SH CONFIGURATION
An optional JSON file,
.IR ~/.config/tailmon.json ,
overrides any of the built\-in defaults:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
  "users": {"gpu\-box": "alice"},
  "default_user": "admin",
  "identity": "~/.ssh/id_ed25519",
  "exclude": ["printer\-pi"],
  "sample_seconds": 1.0,
  "discover_seconds": 30.0,
  "temp_high": {"disk": 55, "nvme": 70},
  "temp_warn_margin": 10.0,
  "gate_key": "~/.ssh/tailmon_gate",
  "gate_hosts": ["asus", "mac\-mini"]
}
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B users
SSH user per Tailscale hostname.
.TP
.B default_user
SSH user for hosts not listed in
.BR users .
Default
.BR null :
whatever
.BR ssh (1)
would pick (\fI~/.ssh/config\fR or your local username).
.TP
.B identity
SSH private key passed with
.BR \-i .
Default
.BR null :
your usual keys or agent.
.TP
.B exclude
Tailscale hostnames never to probe or search.
.TP
.B sample_seconds
Measurement window for rates (CPU %, disk and network throughput) in snapshot mode. Default 1.0.
.TP
.B discover_seconds
In watch mode, how often to re\-run
.B tailscale status
to find new or departed hosts. Default 30.0.
.TP
.B temp_high
Temperature limits in \(deC by sensor kind (cpu, gpu, disk, other) or chip name (nvme, coretemp,
nvidia, acpitz, ...; a chip beats its kind). They replace the limit the sensor reports. Default {}.
.TP
.B temp_warn_margin
A sensor turns yellow this many \(deC below its limit. Default 10.
.TP
.B gate_key
.B tailmon\(aqs
own SSH key for gated hosts. Default
.IR ~/.ssh/tailmon_gate .
.TP
.B gate_hosts
Hosts reached through their gate; kept up to date by
.BR "tailmon gate install" " and " remove .
Default [].
.SH SSH
.B tailmon
runs
.BI "ssh " short\-name
with the Tailscale address pinned as
.BR HostName ,
so
.BI "Host " short\-name
entries in
.I ~/.ssh/config
.RB ( User ,
.BR IdentityFile ,
\&...) apply as they would for a plain
.BR "ssh short\-name" .
Connections use
.B BatchMode=yes
(never prompt for a password) and
.BR StrictHostKeyChecking=accept\-new ,
so key\-based, non\-interactive access is required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
A snapshot exits with status 3 when a temperature sensor is at or above its limit, 0 otherwise.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, disable colors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tailmon.json
Optional configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/config
Per\-host SSH settings, honored as described above.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/tailmon_gate
The gate key (see
.BR gate ).
.TP
.IR ~/.local/share/tailmon/gate.py ", " ~/.config/tailmon/approved ", " ~/.config/tailmon/gate.log
On a gated host: the gate, its approved SHA\-256 list and its log.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
On the machine running
.BR tailmon :
Python 3.6 or later, the
.BR tailscale (1)
CLI logged in to the tailnet, and
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
On each monitored host: Linux with
.B python3
3.6 or later on the
.BR PATH ;
macOS hosts are not monitored, but
.BR search ", " grep ", " mount ", " volume " and " gate
work on them with Remote Login on and the Command Line Tools or Homebrew
.BR python3 ;
searching protected folders such as
.I ~/Documents
needs \(lqAllow full disk access for remote users\(rq in the Remote Login settings.
Optionally on Linux:
.BR nvidia\-smi (1),
.BR who (1),
.BR sinfo (1)
and
.BR squeue (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B tailmon
One snapshot of every online Linux node.
.TP
.B tailmon \-w 1
Live view, refreshed every second.
.TP
.B tailmon \-\-hosts web\-1,db\-1
Only these two hosts.
.TP
.B tailmon \-\-json
Machine\-readable snapshot.
.TP
.B tailmon \-w 60 \-\-log m.jsonl \-\-quiet
Record one sample per host per minute without drawing, e.g.\& from a systemd service.
.TP
.B tailmon \-w 5 \-\-alert \(aqnotify\-send \-u critical "$TAILMON_HOST is hot" "$TAILMON_SENSOR $TAILMON_TEMP"\(aq
Live view with a desktop notification when anything overheats.
.TP
.B tailmon search /var/log \-\-name \(aq*.log\(aq
Find log files on every peer.
.TP
.B tailmon grep \(aqERROR|WARN\(aq /var/log \-i
Grep file contents across the tailnet.
.TP
.B tailmon grep \-\-fixed\-strings needle /home \-\-hosts server\-1 \-\-json
Literal search on one host, as JSON.
.TP
.B tailmon mount
Mount the root filesystem of every peer at
.IR /media/tailscale/ host .
.TP
.B tailmon umount
Unmount them again.
